#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e4cc25e-b0e9-5935-86f7-c450c990cef8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Inverse Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "200cec1b-b266-557f-a2ef-db02cf6d941a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3251" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arcsin (0.2)$ in degrees to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arcsin (0.2) &= (11.536...)^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx 12^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81772ee2-6b04-559c-85a3-976fd12bbd74" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Angles Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "01ba80ea-e3d7-5372-9732-d721f039bae8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3252" :question/problem "If $\\cos\\theta=0.64$ and $\\tan\\alpha=1.25$, where $\\theta$ and $\\alpha$ are acute angles measured in degrees, find $\\theta+\\alpha$ in degrees to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\theta$ and $\\alpha$ we use $\\arccos$ and $\\arctan$ respectively. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\cos\\theta & = 0.64\\ \\arccos(\\cos\\theta) & = \\arccos(0.64) \\ \\theta&=\\arccos(0.64) \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\tan\\alpha & = 1.25 \\ \\arctan(\\tan\\alpha) & =\\arctan(1.25) \\ \\alpha&=\\arctan(1.25). \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\theta + \\alpha & =\\arccos(0.64)+\\arctan(1.25) \\\\ &=(50.208...)^{\\circ} + (51.340...)^{\\circ} \\\\ & = (101.548...)^{\\circ} \\\\ & \\approx 102^{\\circ} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\n**Watch out!** If we round $\\theta$ and $\\alpha$ to the nearest integer, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta \\approx 50^\\circ\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\alpha \\approx 51^\\circ\n$$\n\nAfter we sum up the angles we get an incorrect result:\n\n$$\nθ + α \\mid \\approx 50^{∘} + 51^{∘} = 101^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe correct answer is $102^\\circ$, not $101^\\circ$. This is a rounding error! To avoid this kind of error we have to round *after* we take the sum, not before."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8ccd79e-56a6-58fc-917c-8dc660ad2425" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given the Lengths of Two Sides of a Right Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "7a4aa28f-8a0b-556f-b6ce-beb674cd0234" :question/math-academy-id "e-3253" :question/problem "In a right triangle $\\triangle ABC$, we have $AC = 9$, $BC=13$, and $m\\angle A = \\alpha$. Given that $\\overline{AB}$ is the hypotenuse of the triangle, find the value of $\\alpha$ (rounded to one decimal place)." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the triangle that's described.\n\n![](images/29/29ab71a468b9a57842e07ef74b8d8d770c7ebfacabb3735694c0dd40303ad220.png)\n\nSince we are given the lengths of the adjacent and opposite sides, we can use tangent to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\alpha= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{adjacent}} = \\dfrac{13}{9}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $\\alpha$ using the inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\tan \\alpha &= \\dfrac{13}{9} \\\\[5pt] \\arctan(\\tan \\alpha) &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{13}{9} \\right) \\\\[5pt] \\alpha &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{13}{9} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=(55.304\\,8...)^\\circ \\\\[5pt] &\\approx 55.3^\\circ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}]}
